1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:01,250
Hold your breath.

2
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:02,270
Right now...

3
00:00:02,270 --> 00:00:03,217
with him.

4
00:00:03,217 --> 00:00:03,618
I'll tell you

5
00:00:03,618 --> 00:00:05,020
what your body's doing,

6
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:06,836
every second...

7
00:00:06,836 --> 00:00:07,837
all the way to a man

8
00:00:07,837 --> 00:00:08,600
who held it for

9
00:00:08,645 --> 00:00:10,800
twenty-nine minutes.

10
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:12,796
Ten seconds.

11
00:00:12,796 --> 00:00:13,633
Easy.

12
00:00:13,670 --> 00:00:15,111
It's Pip's birthday,

13
00:00:15,111 --> 00:00:15,709
and she bet him

14
00:00:15,709 --> 00:00:17,080
she'd last longer.

15
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:17,880
Loser gets

16
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:18,967
no cake.

17
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:22,243
Twenty.

18
00:00:22,243 --> 00:00:23,129
Your blood is still

19
00:00:23,129 --> 00:00:24,334
almost totally full

20
00:00:24,334 --> 00:00:25,320
of oxygen.

21
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:26,490
But every second,

22
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:28,357
your body makes exhaust...

23
00:00:28,357 --> 00:00:28,880
and it's got

24
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,100
nowhere to go.

25
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:32,590
Thirty.

26
00:00:32,890 --> 00:00:33,853
Feel that?

27
00:00:33,853 --> 00:00:34,833
That burn?

28
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:35,280
That's not

29
00:00:35,201 --> 00:00:36,600
the missing oxygen.

30
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:38,183
It's mostly the exhaust.

31
00:00:38,183 --> 00:00:39,106
It makes your blood

32
00:00:39,106 --> 00:00:40,733
a tiny bit sour...

33
00:00:40,770 --> 00:00:41,499
and a sensor

34
00:00:41,499 --> 00:00:42,579
at the base of your brain

35
00:00:42,579 --> 00:00:43,550
tastes it...

36
00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:44,933
and pulls the alarm.

37
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:47,550
Forty-five.

38
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:48,333
He's out.

39
00:00:50,170 --> 00:00:51,060
And Pip?

40
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:52,720
Still going...

41
00:00:52,901 --> 00:00:54,320
through her nose.

42
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:55,471
And you?

43
00:00:55,770 --> 00:00:56,890
Breathe.

44
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:58,100
Seriously.

45
00:00:58,130 --> 00:00:59,226
Most people let go

46
00:00:59,226 --> 00:01:00,448
between thirty seconds

47
00:01:00,448 --> 00:01:01,267
and a minute...

48
00:01:01,321 --> 00:01:02,253
with their blood still

49
00:01:02,253 --> 00:01:04,433
more than ninety percent full.

50
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:05,654
You didn't run out.

51
00:01:05,654 --> 00:01:06,822
Your body rings the alarm

52
00:01:06,822 --> 00:01:08,140
on the exhaust...

53
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:08,810
long before

54
00:01:08,810 --> 00:01:10,659
the oxygen gets low.

55
00:01:10,690 --> 00:01:11,702
Round two.

56
00:01:11,702 --> 00:01:13,140
He's seen a trick online:

57
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:14,833
breathe really fast first.

58
00:01:16,004 --> 00:01:16,844
Pip got a whistle

59
00:01:16,844 --> 00:01:18,233
for her birthday.

60
00:01:18,269 --> 00:01:19,400
And she's right.

61
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:20,034
Fast breathing

62
00:01:20,034 --> 00:01:21,700
doesn't add oxygen.

63
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:22,359
It just blows

64
00:01:22,359 --> 00:01:23,918
the exhaust away...

65
00:01:23,918 --> 00:01:25,690
so the alarm rings late.

66
00:01:25,690 --> 00:01:26,840
Sometimes...

67
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:27,890
too late.

68
00:01:27,924 --> 00:01:29,080
The slow way, then.

69
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:30,310
Hand on your belly.

70
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:31,150
Breathe in.

71
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:32,240
Feel it push out?

72
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:33,552
That's your diaphragm,

73
00:01:33,552 --> 00:01:34,631
a dome of muscle

74
00:01:34,631 --> 00:01:35,858
under your lungs.

75
00:01:35,858 --> 00:01:36,833
Remember it.

76
00:01:36,862 --> 00:01:38,066
Sixty seconds:

77
00:01:38,066 --> 00:01:39,400
a new record.

78
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:39,966
And Pip has

79
00:01:39,966 --> 00:01:41,867
a cold, wet cloth.

80
00:01:42,658 --> 00:01:43,929
Cold water on the face

81
00:01:43,929 --> 00:01:45,854
flips an ancient switch.

82
00:01:45,854 --> 00:01:47,277
His heart slows down,

83
00:01:47,277 --> 00:01:48,767
to save oxygen...

84
00:01:48,806 --> 00:01:49,689
the same switch

85
00:01:49,689 --> 00:01:50,760
a seal flips

86
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:52,300
on every dive.

87
00:01:52,332 --> 00:01:53,901
And his spleen squeezes out

88
00:01:53,901 --> 00:01:55,567
spare red blood cells:

89
00:01:55,567 --> 00:01:56,551
oxygen carriers

90
00:01:56,551 --> 00:01:58,200
he didn't know he had.

91
00:01:58,242 --> 00:01:59,248
Ninety.

92
00:01:59,248 --> 00:02:00,800
And here it comes.

93
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:03,070
That dome of muscle...

94
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:04,500
starts kicking.

95
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:05,933
On its own.

96
00:02:05,987 --> 00:02:07,700
Why does it hurt so much?

97
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:09,330
In the nineteen-sixties,

98
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:10,740
two London scientists

99
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:11,859
paralysed themselves

100
00:02:11,859 --> 00:02:12,790
with a drug,

101
00:02:12,790 --> 00:02:13,599
while a machine

102
00:02:13,599 --> 00:02:15,133
breathed for them.

103
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:17,219
With no muscle left to kick,

104
00:02:17,219 --> 00:02:18,404
they went four minutes

105
00:02:18,404 --> 00:02:19,837
without a breath,

106
00:02:19,837 --> 00:02:20,730
calm...

107
00:02:20,730 --> 00:02:21,530
until their doctor

108
00:02:21,530 --> 00:02:22,683
stopped it.

109
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:24,006
Scientists still argue

110
00:02:24,006 --> 00:02:25,100
about what it proves.

111
00:02:25,904 --> 00:02:27,830
One minute fifty-two!

112
00:02:27,830 --> 00:02:29,064
Nearly three times

113
00:02:29,064 --> 00:02:30,690
his first try.

114
00:02:30,989 --> 00:02:31,629
Pip has started

115
00:02:31,629 --> 00:02:32,929
on the cake.

116
00:02:33,049 --> 00:02:33,943
So how far

117
00:02:33,943 --> 00:02:35,267
can a human go?

118
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:36,710
The official record,

119
00:02:36,710 --> 00:02:38,370
on one breath of air:

120
00:02:38,370 --> 00:02:39,341
eleven minutes

121
00:02:39,341 --> 00:02:41,218
and thirty-five seconds...

122
00:02:41,218 --> 00:02:43,200
face down in a pool.

123
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:44,210
With a trick,

124
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:46,176
breathing pure oxygen first,

125
00:02:46,176 --> 00:02:47,278
a man in Croatia

126
00:02:47,278 --> 00:02:49,233
went twenty-nine minutes.

127
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:51,014
That's this whole video...

128
00:02:51,014 --> 00:02:53,800
almost seven times.

129
00:02:53,824 --> 00:02:54,421
And a whale

130
00:02:54,421 --> 00:02:55,333
once stayed down

131
00:02:55,378 --> 00:02:56,299
three hours

132
00:02:56,299 --> 00:02:58,210
and forty-two minutes.

133
00:02:58,210 --> 00:02:59,010
How?

134
00:02:59,010 --> 00:03:00,250
Scientists are still

135
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:01,533
working it out.

136
00:03:03,292 --> 00:03:04,700
But you're not a whale.

137
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:05,387
So what happens

138
00:03:05,387 --> 00:03:07,040
if you hold it too long?

139
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:08,440
Remember the whistle?

140
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:10,343
Breathe fast first,

141
00:03:10,343 --> 00:03:11,788
and your oxygen can run out

142
00:03:11,788 --> 00:03:13,584
before the alarm rings.

143
00:03:13,584 --> 00:03:14,566
You just...

144
00:03:14,566 --> 00:03:15,533
switch off.

145
00:03:16,831 --> 00:03:17,800
In water,

146
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,833
that's how strong swimmers drown.

147
00:03:19,870 --> 00:03:21,880
So: never in water,

148
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:23,160
never alone,

149
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:26,230
and never fast breathing first.

150
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:27,189
On land?

151
00:03:27,189 --> 00:03:28,580
Your body won't let you.

152
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:29,639
Even if you blacked out,

153
00:03:29,639 --> 00:03:30,840
it would take over...

154
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:32,599
and breathe for you.

155
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:33,454
Count your breaths

156
00:03:33,454 --> 00:03:35,570
for fifteen seconds.

157
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:37,100
Starting...

158
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:38,067
now.

159
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:53,655
About four?

160
00:03:53,655 --> 00:03:54,243
You didn't choose

161
00:03:54,243 --> 00:03:55,400
a single one.

162
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:56,331
That part of his brain

163
00:03:56,331 --> 00:03:57,460
he was fighting?

164
00:03:57,460 --> 00:03:58,499
It's been breathing for him

165
00:03:58,499 --> 00:04:00,170
his whole life.

166
00:04:00,230 --> 00:04:01,344
So this time,

167
00:04:01,344 --> 00:04:03,324
when Pip takes a big breath...

168
00:04:03,324 --> 00:04:05,560
he lets the birthday girl win.

169
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:07,090
Turns out...

170
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:08,225
this is one contest

171
00:04:08,225 --> 00:04:09,600
you're supposed to lose.

172
00:04:12,590 --> 00:04:13,787
Next week:

173
00:04:13,814 --> 00:04:15,251
what happens if you step

174
00:04:15,251 --> 00:04:18,370
into space without a suit.

175
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:19,620
Subscribe...

176
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:21,702
and keep breathing.

